Z||000022||Mattyman||11-20-2001||10:55 PM||||When I was looking for a Fiero I came across one that had a Grand National engine in it.  It was the turbo V-6.  That car flew.  I got a ride in it and it felt like the front end was gonna come off the ground.  He had the slips in it and it ran the 1/4 mile in the 13's.  That is a sweet engine for a fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Raydar||11-20-2001||11:15 PM||||The turbo 3.8 that came in the GN and the Trans Am will not bolt to the Fiero tranny. It's the B-O-P bolt pattern. You'd need to find an adapter. Advance Adapters might have one.<BR>Hugh, Are you thinking about Kenne-Bell for the mods? Seems like they used to do a lot of Buick 3.8 tweaks. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000024||hugh||11-21-2001||07:34 AM||||Raydar,I only got my front air dams from Kenne Bell.Kenne bell used to give an et improvement for each part.If you bought them all,you would have the only street car that would run in the 4's.If anyone is interested,Archie said if he had enough orders he would make more kits for the Fiero and the GN engine. ||||reg||14
Z||000025||Formula88||11-21-2001||11:33 AM||||I bought my 87 Grand National new in the Fall of 87.  The 86's and 87's were the only ones to have the SFI injected, 3.8L V6 turbo with intercooler.  Earlier models either didn't have the SFI injection, intercooler, etc.  They came out in the late 70's and a turbo 3.8 V6 with a carb, but weren't good performers till 86.  In 86 they were rated at 235HP, in 87 they were rated at 245HP.  Both engines were identcial, and both put out at least 245HP stock.  The GNX's had 16" wheels and tires (beautiful cross lace rims, similar to GT/Formula rims) and the big deal was the ceramic impeller on the turbo - supposed to eliminate most all turbo lag.  The GNX was rated at 300HP, but the actual performance difference between the two was minimal.  The GN was stronger than it's rating, and the GNX might have been rated lightly high.<P>Either way, with a chip & thermostat, you're pushing almost 300HP anyway, cuz the chip controls the boost pressure.  I was running 19psi in mine and toasted SS 396 Chevelles and new (87-88) Corvettes without much problem.  Heat was the key.  It was discovered that each degree you lower the intake charge temp on a GN is worth about 1 HP.  So you have 50 more HP with an intake air temp of 20*F than you do at 70*F.  And that's with the intercooler!<P>Since this is a rear wheel drive engine, and not the same bolt pattern as the 2.8, it would require lots of fabrication to install in a Fiero.  Still, with enough money, talent, and time anything is possible.  And yes, in 87, the GN was faster than the 87 Corvette.  Something the Chevy guys weren't too happy about.  In 88 the Regal platform went to FWD, so the GN was canned.  Whether the Vette had anything to do with that is unknown.  The GN engine wasn't suited to a FWD platform....so it quietly went away, with it's last hurrah in the 89 Turbo Trans Am 20th Anniversary Edition.  <P>Interesting side note, as of 1999, when the 30th Anniversary TA came out, the 89 Turbo TA was the fastest Trans Am ever released, in stock form.  Faster than the 69 Ram Air IV, faster than the 73 SD455.  It's also the only V6 TA ever made.  To my knowledge, it still holds the title as fastest production Trans Am.  How I'd love to find one of those!  With the V6 weight, the car handled even better. <P>The only car I've ever had that was faster than my Grand National was my 72 455HO Trans Am.  It was close, but the TA was slightly faster in the quarter mile.  But the TA also had a fresh engine with a not quite stock cam, but close to original specs - just a more modern design.  It actually idled smoother than the stock cam.  The GN could have been easily made to be faster than my TA with just a little bit of work.||||reg||6
Z||000026||NEPTUNE||11-21-2001||11:47 AM||||I believe this tire smokin MF actually cost less than a Fiero GT. They ain't pretty (to me) but they were (are) VERY FAST! An engine swap would be similar to any other 3.8 Buford into a Fiero, but the engine itself would cost a lot because of the scarcity and collector value.||||reg||1
Z||000027||LoW_KeY||11-21-2001||11:59 AM||||Yep, I've seen a documentation of the GNX (grand national) on speedvision and they said they got help from McClaren doing the intercooler and all when they did that it boosted the performance.<P>you know they were sticking 3.8's in these suckers why didn't they do something like this to a fiero? 3.8 then have shelby or someone along that line work on one.. the fiero would have been a beast! for it having a small V6 I still think it is.. I've toasted a lot of cars and have been toasted but only by a car length.  That was with the 2.8 though the 3.4 rocks.<P>Like I said though I'm still curious why the 3.8 never made it to the fiero I mean its in my 85 buick winter beater!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kc.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000028||BlackStang95||11-21-2001||06:59 PM||||1985   Buick Regal Grand National             7.5            15.7<P>1986   Buick Regal Grand National             4.9            13.9<P>Incredibly fast for an 80s car, especially a v-6.  I'd think a fiero with that 86 engine in it would do better than 13's...||||reg||1
Z||000029||banditbalz||11-21-2001||07:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LoW_KeY:<BR><B>I've toasted a lot of cars and have been toasted but only by a car length.  That was with the 2.8 though the 3.4 rocks.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LoW_KeY, you have any time slips for your 3.4? Just curious as to the  acceleration diff. <BR>I have a friend with a '97 Grand National, minor mods like a fuel pressure reg. and injectors. Just when I thought my 86 GT with 4 speed was the fastest thing on 4 wheels, I pulled up beside him at a set of red lights. Now nothing, absolutely nothing, has ever taken me from one set of lights to the next. Light turned green and all I seen was tailights. This is a 4000lb car! I consistantly pull 1.9  60 foot times and this Regal walked away from me. Later in the summer I raced him at Cayuga Dragway Park and he posted a 13.4 to my 15.3<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/blackwhite2_3.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||1
Z||000030||hugh||11-21-2001||07:54 PM||||If the transmission would hold the torque,just my engine in a Fiero would probably run in the 10's.I've run low 12's at 115 and my GN weighs at least 700lbs. more than my Fiero.||||reg||14
